%S A401747 1,3,6,9,15,21,25,31,45,51,66,76,87,105,110,120,148,171,183,199,217,
%T A401747 243,276,297,308,351,371,385,435,465,471,486,561,583,630,639,686,733,
%U A401747 780,807,861,869,931,956,1002,1042,1117,1150,1191,1275,1326,1338,1431,1449,1521,1553,1640,1663
%N A401747 Number of jumps until the n-th return to 0 of the walk on the nonnegative integers that starts at 0 and, on its j-th visit to x, jumps to x - j if j <= x and to j - x - 1 if j > x.
%C A401747 The starting position counts as the first visit to 0, but not as a return. Only landings count as subsequent visits, including a jump from x back to x. Equivalently, the walk moves on cells 0, 1, 2, ... and, on its j-th visit to a cell, jumps j cells toward a wall at the left edge of cell 0, reflecting at the wall.
%C A401747 The walk returns to 0 infinitely often, and before the n-th return it stays in [0, n-1].
%C A401747 a(n) <= n*(n+1)/2, with equality if and only if n is a Queneau number (A054639); equivalently, the permutation i -> min(2*i, 2*n+1-2*i) of {1, ..., n}, which is the inverse of the Queneau-Daniel permutation, is a single cycle; this also holds if and only if GF(2^n) has a type-2 optimal normal basis over GF(2) (see A054639).
%C A401747 The statements in the two preceding comments and the formulas are proved in the Proofs link.
%C A401747 There are 10085 values of n <= 100000 with a(n) = n*(n+1)/2.
%C A401747 For n <= 100000, the maximum of (n*(n+1)/2 - a(n))/n is 35925/16384 = 2.19268798828125, attained only at n = 16384, where a(n) = 134189995.
%C A401747 A399658 gives the corresponding numbers for the walk on the integers that starts at 0 and, on its j-th visit to x, jumps to x - j if x > 0 and to x + j if x <= 0. A399658(n) <= n*(n+1), with equality if and only if 2*n+1 is in A001122.
%C A401747 A358338 gives the positions of the analogous walk on the nonnegative integers that, on its j-th visit to x, moves to |x - j|; in terms of cells, that walk reflects at the center of cell 0 rather than at its left edge.
%H A401747 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401747/b401747.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A401747 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401747/a401747.png">Plot of (n*(n+1)/2 - a(n))/n for n = 1..100000</a>.
%H A401747 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401747/a401747.txt">Proofs of the bound and of the Queneau criterion</a>.
%H A401747 Alper Ferudun, <a href="/A401747/a401747_1.txt">Rust program</a>.
%F A401747 a(n) <= n*(n-1)/2 + A003558(n) <= n*(n+1)/2, where A003558(n) is the least m > 0 such that 2^m == +-1 (mod 2*n+1).
%F A401747 a(2^k) <= 2^(2*k-1) - 3*2^(k-1) + (k^2+3*k+4)/2 for k >= 1; hence limsup_{n->infinity} (n*(n+1)/2 - a(n))/n >= 2.
%F A401747 If n is in A054639, then a(n+1) = n*(n+1)/2 + A003558(n+1).
%e A401747 The first visit to 0 gives a jump to 1 - 0 - 1 = 0, so a(1) = 1.
%e A401747 Next, the second visit to 0 gives a jump to 2 - 0 - 1 = 1, and the first visit to 1 gives a jump to 1 - 1 = 0. Thus the positions through the second return are 0, 0, 1, 0, and a(2) = 3.
%e A401747 The positions through the fourth return are 0, 0, 1, 0, 2, 1, 0, 3, 2, 0, so a(3) = 6 = 3*4/2 and a(4) = 9 < 4*5/2. Here the permutation in the comments has cycles (1,2,4) and (3), so 4 is not a Queneau number. Also 2^3 == -1 (mod 9), so A003558(4) = 3, and a(4) = 4*3/2 + 3 attains the bound in the first formula.
%o A401747 (Rust) // See Ferudun link.
